{
	title: 'Three delivery tiers: Alt-Enter queues, Enter interjects, Ctrl-Enter interrupts',
	spec: "A prompt sent while a turn runs has one delivery value (Delivery in protocol.ts): 'queue' (Alt-Enter, tier 3) runs after the turn; 'interject' (Enter, tier 2) joins the inbox without aborting the stream or running tools, and the turn delivers it before its next request (continuing after a finished answer to deliver it); 'interrupt' (Ctrl-Enter, tier 1, and the web Steer button) stops the stream and running tools (unsafe-to-stop calls finish first, task ker) and the turn goes on with it. The submit command, drafts and waiting uploads carry delivery; a submit without it interrupts, and the legacy queue: true still means 'queue'. On an idle, paused or failed session the Enter keys behave alike. Each delivered message keeps its tier: the transcript title and the model's header say interrupting or interjecting (inbox interject field). While a turn runs with queued messages, an empty Enter sends the oldest one at the next round (/queue next) and an empty Ctrl-Enter interrupts with it (/queue now): the host clears its queue flag in place, keeping id, text and order. Undo (Cmd-Z, Ctrl-/) on an empty prompt with no text undo sends /queue undo, which queues the last early-sent message again if the turn has not read it yet, and otherwise says so. Web and terminal behave the same; the help rows and key help name the keys. In a terminal, Ctrl-Enter and Cmd-Z need modified-key reporting (kitty keyboard protocol, e.g. Ghostty).",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'The flag is only sent while the session is busy, so idle submits stay unchanged. A nextRound steer after Escape still swaps the controller so the stopped turn goes on, as an interrupting steer does. While a turn runs, the help row drops shift-enter: with ctrl-enter added, it truncated esc: pause at 80 columns. Uploads waiting to finish resume as plain Enter, so a Ctrl-Enter sent during an upload becomes a between-rounds steer.',
		'Promotion reuses /queue next, so slash-command plumbing carried it with no new protocol command. /queue undo remembers only the last early-sent id per session in host memory (prompts.promoted); a host restart forgets it, which only loses an undo. The interrupting tier keeps the stored field name steering for old history.'
